---
title: ModMox gets an opponent
blurb: The rules engine learns to change events before they happen, an AI shows up to play against, and the whole thing finally gets a screen you can click on.
date: 2026-11-11
tags: [game]
---

When I [last wrote about ModMox](/blog/a-modular-magic-engine), the engine could play
a small game of Alpha-era Magic with nobody watching and nobody on the other side of
the table. There was no opponent, no screen, and the next thing on the to-do list was
replacement and prevention effects. Five days later, all three of those are different.
The engine got its tenth milestone, an functional AI has been implemented, and as of this morning I
can sit down, attack with a Shivan Dragon, and watch the other side decide whether to
chump block it.

The work fell into four major chunks:

## Changing things before they happen

Milestone 10 landed the same morning the last post went up. It covers the corner of
Magic where an effect doesn't react to something, it rewrites it. Fog doesn't undo
combat damage, it makes the damage never happen. A regeneration shield doesn't bring a
creature back, it swaps "destroy this" for "tap it and pull it out of combat." The
first cards to use it were Fog, Samite Healer, White Knight, Black Knight, Drudge
Skeletons and Terror.

The good news was that the event pipeline from milestone 2 already had a slot for
this. Every change to the game was already being offered to replacement effects before
it applied, there just weren't any. Now that step can turn one event into none, one or
several. Fully prevented damage becomes nothing at all, which matters, because damage
that was never dealt can't trigger anything or carry deathtouch along with it.

The bad news was the ordering rule. When two replacement effects want the same event,
the affected player picks which goes first, and the engine never waits for input. That
choice can come up halfway through a batch of simultaneous events, like the middle of
combat damage. So a batch that needs an answer now stops where it is and gets stored
in the game state as a Suspended Batch, and it carries on once the player answers.
Making that work meant changing what an effect is. Effects used to perform their
events directly. Now they only propose them, one batch per step, and a procedure walks
through the proposals and can stop between any two. Because the suspended batch is
part of the state, it gets copied and checkpointed like everything else, so undo works
even in the middle of one.

The engine also only asks when the order could matter. If there's one effect, or the
effects are interchangeable, or they'd all prevent everything anyway, it quietly picks
the oldest. Nobody wants a dialog asking which of two identical Fogs should go first.

Protection came along in the same milestone, since "protection from black" is mostly a
prevention effect with a Filter attached, plus indestructible and mana in activation
costs. The suite reached 386 tests and caught 35 of 36 deliberate rule mutations. The
one that got away was "each effect applies only once per event," which no card in the
game can show the difference for yet.

## Someone to play against

Milestone 11 was the first playable slice: three 40-card decks and an AI to pilot
them. The engine calls the two players the Hero and the Villain, and whatever answers
decisions for one of them is an Agent. The AI lives in its own addon,
`addons/modmox_ai/`, which depends on the engine and is never referenced by it.

The design borrows from Forge, the long-running open source Magic client, whose AI is
a big pile of heuristics, refined by simulation. I borrowed the ideas, but none of the
code, since Forge is GPL. The more interesting difference is what I got to skip. Forge
needs around 155 hand-written classes, one per kind of effect, to tell its AI what
each effect does. ModMox, on the other hand, just asks the engine. The AI copies the game, tries a play on
the copy, and scores the board that comes out. That means it has no card names in it
and no authored hints, which is the whole point in a game where players rewrite cards.
If your Lightning Bolt hits every creature now, the AI finds that out the same way it
finds out everything else, by trying it.

The part I cared most about was that the AI doesn't cheat. It never gets handed the
real game. It gets what its player could legally see, plus a World, which is a copy of
the game where every hidden card has been dealt out again at random. The Villain knows
what could be in your hand, never what is. There's a test for this that I like a lot:
set up two games that differ only in where the hidden cards are, and check that the AI
sees exactly the same thing in both.

Its randomness is careful in a similar way. Each decision gets a fresh random stream
derived from the encounter's seed and how many decisions came before, because a save
is only the setup plus the list of answers. A running stream would be lost on reload,
the AI would answer differently the second time, and you could reload until it
misplayed. Search budgets are counted in samples and never in milliseconds for the
same reason. A fast machine and a slow one get the same Villain.

Milestone 12 put Flat Monte Carlo on top. On a priority decision the AI takes its best
few candidates and plays each one forward through the rest of the turn and all of the
next, on the same set of imagined worlds, and it only switches away from its first
instinct if another option clearly wins. Difficulty sets how much searching happens
and nothing else. Easy doesn't search at all, Normal compares four candidates over
four samples, and Hard uses twelve samples. A Normal decision takes a median of 358
milliseconds. Difficulty never adds mistakes, and Villains never concede, because
finishing someone off is part of the fun.

Here's the honest part. I set a bar for this milestone: Normal had to beat Easy at
least 55% of the time over 100 pairs of seeded games. It scored 52.5%, 105 wins to 95,
which is statistically indistinguishable from a coin flip. Digging in, the search only
changed the AI's mind in about 5% of decisions, and combat, where most of these games
get decided, runs on the same planners at every difficulty. In 85 of the 100 pairs
each side won once, so the deck matchup and who went first mattered more than who was
thinking harder. I wrote the result down and moved on instead of tuning forever. The
likely fix is letting the AI roll out its attack options too, and that can wait until
there's a game around it.

My favorite bug from this stretch was the AI casting Dark Ritual for no reason. Three
black mana that empties unspent should score the same as doing nothing, but in a
rollout it changed which lands were left untapped, and that nudged the score above
passing. Now a spell that only makes mana is considered only if the mana unlocks
something the AI couldn't already do.

## What a card looks like

With something to play against, milestone 13 is the GUI, and it's big enough that it's
split into nine parts. The first was drawing cards.

There are two ways a card gets drawn. A Card Face is the full card, with frame, name,
cost, type line, rules text and power and toughness, used anywhere you read a card. A
Battlefield Face is what a permanent looks like in play: the art, the name, a large
power and toughness box, and a small chip for each keyword it has. Each chip shows a
16 by 16 pixel icon, so you can tell a flier from a first striker at a glance. The
icons are built from a text grid by a script, and can be touched up by hand in
Aseprite afterward.

The frames aren't images of finished cards. The game works out which layers a card
needs from its characteristics, a frame for its colors, a box if it's a creature, a
crown if it's legendary, and bakes them together. That matters for the same reason
everything else here does. A card that gains a color mid-run has to get the right
frame without anybody drawing one. Both kinds of face are drawn from one small object
describing how the card currently looks, so the deck screen and the battlefield can't
disagree. Art is the card's first printing, fetched by a script that records the
artist credits as it goes.

## A screen you can click

The rest of the week went into the Encounter Screen. The rule that shaped it is that
exactly one object in the GUI is allowed to touch the engine. That's the Encounter
Presenter. It runs the encounter, and after every change it hands the screen a
snapshot of what the Hero is allowed to see and what they're being asked. The screen
draws from the snapshot and nothing else, so it can't accidentally show you the
Villain's hand, because it never has it. A test scans the GUI code and fails if any
other file so much as mentions the engine by name.

The presenter also runs the Villain on a worker thread, since a Hard decision can take
over a second and the window shouldn't freeze while it thinks. I learned why the
ordering there matters from the throwaway prototype, which once read the game while
the AI was halfway through recalculating layers and crashed on a half-computed
creature. The real version takes its snapshot before the AI starts thinking and
refuses every command until it's done. Pausing works the same way. Escape doesn't
stop the Villain's thought, it just doesn't collect the answer until you resume.

The first version of the screen answered every decision through a debug panel full of
lists, which worked but was no fun at all. So each kind of decision is getting its own
input mode, and a kind isn't finished until its debug form is deleted. What
a click on anything means is worked out in one place, and the Glow, the colored
outline on things you can choose, is drawn from that same answer. Orange-red is a
target, blue is a mana source, green is anything else. Since both come from one
source, something that glows always takes a click, and something that doesn't glow is
silent instead of throwing an error at you.

Combat was this morning's work, and it needed a different idea. Declaring attackers is
one answer for the whole decision, so a single click can't be the answer. Clicks build
up a Combat Selection instead, and the pass button turns into "Attack with 2" to
confirm it. Attackers step forward into a lane across the middle of the board. A
blocker gets a line with an arrowhead to whatever it's blocking, pale gold while
you're choosing and white once it's locked in. I first had blockers slide over to
stand opposite their attackers, but that fought with keeping creatures in a stable
order, so the lines carry the pairing on their own. Dividing damage among several
blockers gets a little minus and plus stepper on each creature, starting from a split
that kills as many as it can.

## Where it stands

ModMox is now a game you can actually play a round of. The card pool is at 41 cards,
there are 22 decision records, and the test count is somewhere around 870 across the
engine, the AI and the GUI. Activating abilities, paying mana, targeting and all of
combat have real inputs. Mulligans, casting from hand and a few others still go
through the debug panel.

Next is the stack, with arrows from spells to their targets and the first animations,
along with slowing the Villain down so you can follow what it's doing. After that come
dragging cards out of your hand, zone viewers, and a card inspector. None of the
roguelike exists yet, but for the first time there's something on the other side of
the table, and it's deciding whether to block.
